# Streamlit
.streamlit/secrets.toml

# =====================================================================
# Project-specific rules for the SEC Filing Assistant
# =====================================================================

# Data: keep large raw filings out of the repo.
# Commit only a small sample under data/sample/.
data/raw/
data/interim/
data/processed/
data/diagnostics/
# Built search indexes: rebuilt from data/processed/, and large.
data/index/
# The chunk-size sweep's copies of the corpus, one per budget with its own
# indexes: rebuilt from data/interim/ by `python -m src.evaluation.chunk_sweep`.
data/sweep/
# The mechanical XBRL benchmark: regenerated from the facts store by
# `python -m src.retrieval benchmark`, and every chunk id in it is invalidated
# by a re-chunk. benchmark/questions.jsonl, for hand-written questions, is not ignored.
benchmark/generated.jsonl
*.zip
*.gz

# Secrets: ignore all .env variants but keep the committed template.
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem

# Vector stores and model artifacts
*.faiss
*.index
chroma/

# OS and editor files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/

# The Streamlit agent skill, when `streamlit skills` or the app's own prompt
# installs it in the project. Both are symlinks into the Streamlit package
# inside .venv, so they resolve only on the machine that made them, and
# Streamlit's installer says not to commit them. The project's own skill,
# .claude/skills/verify, stays tracked.
.agents/skills/developing-with-streamlit
.claude/skills/developing-with-streamlit

# Terminal output saved by src/utils.py run logging. Records of one machine
# run, not shared source.
logs/

# Results and other data files. A script or notebook in this repo writes each
# one, and like logs/ it is a record of one run on one machine, so none is
# committed. Running the code again writes a new file, which is the same as
# the old one only where nothing behind it moved: a hosted model does not
# repeat its answers, and another commit or another build of the indexes ranks
# passages differently. The files behind the numbers in the README were
# committed and then removed, so they are in the history.
# notebooks/removed-results.json lists each with its row count, its SHA-256
# and the git object that holds it, and `git cat-file -p <git_blob>` prints
# one. A results/ folder anywhere holds a run's output.
results/
*.csv
*.tsv
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.parquet
*.feather
*.pkl
*.pickle
*.npy
*.npz
*.jsonl
# Hand-written inputs that no code produces, so they stay in the repo.
!notebooks/test_data/test_questions.csv
!benchmark/questions.jsonl
# The chunk-size sweep's two reported runs (#45). That issue's acceptance
# criteria ask for the result to be committed with the fingerprint of each
# build, so these are the one exception to the rule above: the table of each
# run and its JSON, 47 KB in four files. Everything else under results/ stays
# out, the per-question files of these two runs included.
!/results/
/results/*
!/results/chunk-sweep-bm25-20261007/
/results/chunk-sweep-bm25-20261007/*
!/results/chunk-sweep-bm25-20261007/summary.csv
!/results/chunk-sweep-bm25-20261007/summary.json
!/results/chunk-sweep-fy2025-20261007/
/results/chunk-sweep-fy2025-20261007/*
!/results/chunk-sweep-fy2025-20261007/summary.csv
!/results/chunk-sweep-fy2025-20261007/summary.json
